5,"Walak language resources | Joshua ProjectWalak people - WikipediaThe Living, Dead and Dying: Music of the New Guinea Wape ...The Living, Dead and Dying: Music of the New Guinea Wape",https://joshuaproject.net/languages/wlw,"Listing of people groups speaking Walak. Walak dialects and alternate names. Bible and ministry resource availability. Music uses the goknggaik [id] instrument, and often features the song Wasioayamari (""Let Us Unite""). [6] The dance recounts Papuan ancestors believed to have migrated from Yunnan, via Taiwan, Philippines, Papua New Guinea, and east to Ifala Island, then to Genyem, spreading across Papua. Recorded over an 18-month period spanning 1970 and 1971, anthropologist William E. Mitchell documents a people struggling with famine and disease. To combat their struggle, they hold curing festivals, sing demon chants, and conduct other musical remedies. Listen to The Living, Dead and Dying: Music of the New Guinea Wape on Spotify. Various Artists · Compilation · 1978 · 19 songs.",Walak,Trans-New Guinea
